.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE = Element>
.br
class csmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM< dim, CELLTYPE >"\fBAccumulator\fP and integrator for FEM or FVM-derived integral forms of PDEs and their solution in the form of linear algebraic sets of equations (system Ax=b)\&. Applicable to CSMP++ \fBModel\fP objects or their subregions\&. 


.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthai 

.PP
Stephen G\&. Roberts 

.PP
various extra constributions (Adrian Burri, Julian Mindel, Kho Luat Tran)\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
1997 

.PP
replaced by merge of \fBPDE_Integrator\fP 1/4/22
.RE
.PP
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
To encapsulate the integration and numerical solution of partial-differential equations (PDE) in their integral form\&. This process entails (1) the assembly of a sparse matrix with the contributions from finite elements or finite volumes, (2) the assembly of a right-hand vector with essential and initial conditions; elimination of Dirichlet constraints\&. (3) Inversion of the matrix equation Ax=b to find the solution vector x\&. The dependent physical \fBvariable(s)\fP stored in the solution vector are updated on the mesh\&.
.SH "Design Intent"
.PP
The intent of using a 'strategy' pattern for the design was to separate the PDE operator / integrator implementation from the the type of finite element(s) or FV's used and the solution method chosen\&. As a consequence, the same PDE operator objects and solution methods can be used for a range of computational cell types with different node numbers and so forth\&.

.PP
The \fBPDE_Integrator\fP class is a base class from which specific \fBPDE_Integrator\fP subclasses can be inherited to test improvements of its functionality\&.
.SH "Applicability"
.PP
\fBPDE_Integrator\fP objects can be used to carry out any 2D and 3D computation which are possible with CSMP++\&. These computations may either appy to entire \fBModel\fP objects or to Regions which form subsets thereof\&.
.SH "Participants"
.PP
Each \fBPDE_Integrator\fP contains maps of pointers to left and righthand operators that are inherited from the \fBMathOperatorLHS\fP and \fBMathOperatorRHS\fP base classes\&. Other variables set the state of the algorithm, the time_increment in transient calculations and so forth\&.

.PP
\fBPDE_Integrator\fP also contains or connects to a \fBSolver\fP object which inverts the sparse solution matrix\&.
.SH "Collaborations"
.PP
\fBPDE_Integrator\fP interact with \fBModel\fP, \fBRegion\fP, \fBBoundary\fP and \fBSplitBoundary\fP objects\&. It queries these objects for the data needed to setup the solution matrices and vectors\&.

.PP
\fBPDE_Integrator\fP also collaborates with the \fBMeshManager\fP and the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP to gain access to variables and finite elements (\fBElement\fP class instances)\&.

.PP
\fBPDE_Integrator\fP obtains the finite-element contributions from the \fBElement\fP class that in turn accesses the specific finite-element type which appears hidden behind the specific element that is assembled\&. This is implemented as a 'bridge' pattern\&.
.SH "Consequences"
.PP
The design as a full-function base class implies that to create a PDE operator subclass one only has to overwrite those methods that one wants to modify\&. Other code does not need to be modified\&. To create additional PDE operators, inherit these from \fBMathOperatorLHS\fP or \fBMathOperatorRHS\fP\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is a stand-alone object, which is passed to the \fBModel\fP\&. The interaction with the \fBModel\fP follows a visitor like pattern\&. The \fBModel\fP passes the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP on to a region to which its application has been restricted\&. Inside the \fBModel\fP, the following steps take place when a \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is applied by calling \fBModel::Apply()\fP:

.PP
To start with, the \fBModel\fP checks whether the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP must be handed down to a specific region, boundary or split boundary\&. Then the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP builds or updates its vectors and matrices so that they can store a sufficient number of entries:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::EstablishMatrixSetup( mesh, phys_vars );
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP accumulates the contributions to the global solution matrices seqentially:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::Accumulate ( mesh, property_collection );
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the computation is a transient one, initial conditions are added into the righthand vector:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::AssignInitialConditions( mesh, property_collection );
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then, the boundary conditions are applied to the lefthand and righthand side:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::AssignEssentialConditions( mesh, property_collection );
Here an elimination is performed, retaining, if so, the symmetric shape of the solution matrix\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the computation is a transient one, and the solution method is Backward Euler implicit timestepping, righthand-side source terms (typically fluid source or other rates) are now multiplied with the time increment and added to the righthand vector\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::LateAccumulate( mesh, property_collection );
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now the solver is invoked to invert the solution matrix:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::Solve();
.fi
.PP

.PP
The results from the computation are mapped back into property storage:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::OutputResults( mesh, property_collection );
.fi
.PP

.PP
and post-processing operations are applied:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator::PostProcess( mesh, property_collection );
.fi
.PP

.PP
Post-processing may be for instance, the computation of flow velocities from computed fluid-pressure gradients and permeability values\&.

.PP
Important is also the mapping from global node ID numbers to entry positions in the global solution matrix: In the simplemost case, of a single degree-of-freedom per node and a global computation, the mapping is:

.PP
G(n,n), rh(n) -> n = node-ID-1

.PP
For vector variables:

.PP
G(n,n), rh(n) -> n = (node-ID-1) * dimensions + j

.PP
where j is the index variable for the spatial dimensions x, y, z\&.

.PP
For tensor variables:

.PP
G(n,n), rh(n) -> n = (node-ID-1) * dimensions^2 i*dimensions + j

.PP
where i, j are the index variables corresponding to the rows and column indices of the tensor variable with the i_max = j_max = spatial dimensions\&.

.PP
In the case of \fBRegion\fP computations, an instance of the class IndexMapper transforms the global node ID numbers into the scaled ID numbers 1\&.\&.\&.n-nodes in group\&. Subsequently, the same transformations are applied for vector and tensor variables\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The simple most computation with an \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is a 3D steady-state computation of fluid pressure\&. This involves the definition and instantiation of some PDE operators as first step:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// compute steady\-state fluid pressure from [K]{p} = {q}
PDE_Integrator<2U>  fluid_pressure;

NumIntegral_dNT_op_dN_dV<2U>  conductance( example_model\&.Database(), "conductivity",   "fluid pressure", "fluid pressure" );
NumIntegral_NT_op_N_dV<2U>    source( example_model\&.Database(), "fluid volume source", "fluid pressure" );
VelocityAndVolumeFlux<2U>     velo( example_model, "conductivity", "porosity", "fluid pressure", true );

fluid_pressure\&.Add( &conductance );
fluid_pressure\&.Add( &source );
fluid_pressure\&.AddPostProcess( &velo );
example_model\&.Apply( fluid_pressure );
.fi
.PP

.PP
A more advanced calculation of displacements, strains and stresses is done in the following example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator<2U>  deformation;
if ( restricted_to_rock ) deformation\&.RestrictApplicationTo("rock");

PT_op<2U>                    bforces( p_ref, "force", "displacement" );
NumIntegral_BT_D_B_dV<2U>    stiffness( p_ref, "Young's modulus", "Poisson's ratio", "displacement", "displacement");
if ( with_plane_stress ) stiffness\&.PlaneStress();
NumIntegral_PT_op_dS<2U>     bstresses( p_ref, "Neumann stress", "displacement" );
NumIntegral_PT_op_dV<2U>     bodyforce( p_ref, "gravity force", "displacement");
NumIntegral_BT_D_op_dV<2U>   volstrain( p_ref, "dilatation", "Young's modulus", "Poisson's ratio", "displacement");
NumIntegral_BT_op_dV<2U>     porepressure( p_ref, "fluid pressure", "displacement");

deformation\&.Add( &stiffness );
deformation\&.Add( &bforces );  // must always be there so that Dirichlet conditions are accumulated
if ( with_boundary_stresses ) deformation\&.Add( &bstresses );
if ( with_body_forces )       deformation\&.Add( &bodyforce );
if ( with_volume_strains )    deformation\&.Add( &volstrain );
if ( with_pore_pressure )     deformation\&.Add( &porepressure );

StressesAndStrains<2U>  postpro( example_model, "Young's modulus",
                                                         "Poisson's ratio", "displacement", true, true );

if ( with_plane_stress ) postpro\&.PlaneStress();
 deformation\&.AddPostProcess( &postpro );

example_model\&.Apply( deformation );
.fi
.PP
 
.SH "Constructor & Destructor Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::PDE_Integrator_CRM ()"
Default Constructor

.PP
The default constructor sets the computational domain equivalent to the \fBModel\fP\&. The default \fBSolver\fP is SAMG, without license, you only have access to a single level solver\&. The default computation is a steady-state computation\&. Thus, if you want to carry out a transient calculation you have to use the method \fBTransient()\fP\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
CELLTYPE is used here because DOMAIN caused a clash with DOMAIN defined in <cmath> 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::Accumulate (const \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & gref)\fR [protected]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
accumulates finite element integrals into solution matrix and right-hand side; uses node numbering \fBAccumulate()\fP loops over the finite-elements in the \fBModel\fP or target \fBRegion\fP and accumulates their contributions to the solution matrix and the righthand vector\&. If the computation is part of a finite-difference time-stepping scheme this is accounted for by multiplying time-dependent contributions like source or sink rates with the time-icrement\&. The method retrieves information from the mesh and the property managers\&. If a group computation is carried out, the target group is accessed by a pointer\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The generation and addition of the element contributions to the solution matrix and righthand vector requires the execution of the math operator methods:

.PP
.PP
.nf
GetOperands();
ComputeContribution();
AssignToGlobal();
.fi
.PP

.PP
If a transient calculation is chosen the "grad-test function" terms and the Neumann boundary condition terms are multiplied with the time-increment\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
\fBAccumulate()\fP is executed internally when the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is passed to the \fBModel\fP\&. 
