.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::Add (\fBMathOperatorLHS\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > * op)"

.PP
add desired integral terms (pde operators) to the left-hand side matrix of the algebraic system of equations The \fBPDE_Integrator\fP object maintains a list of pointers to MathOperator instances which represent parts of the PDE which is being modeled by the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP\&. \fBAdd()\fP allows you to associate such operators_ (like dove^2) with the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIop\fP pointer to PDE operator objects (=Operands) which you defined earlier in your program\&. These are always subclasses derived from the MathOperatorLHSptr or MathOperatorRHSptr base classes\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The MathOperator pointers are stored in the lhs_operators_ and rhs_operators_ maps\&. Their functionality is accessed in the accumulation process of the solution matrix G and the righthand vector rh\&. The maps prevent, that multiple instances of the same MathOperator object with the same name are added\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Each \fBPDE_Integrator\fP needs at least one left and one righthand math operator\&. Define these before you pass the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP to the \fBModel\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::AssignEssentialConditions (const \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & domain)\fR [protected]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
modifies right-hand vector; adding Dirichlet condition terms that were elimitated before The Dirichlet constraints have already been eliminated, but their contributions to the non-Dirichlet rows have to be added to the RHS\&.

.PP
The guts of the elimation now live in EnumerateAndFixMatrixSize()\&.

.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
Luat Khoa Tran 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::AssignInitialConditions (const \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & gref)\fR [protected]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
in time-dependent calculations this method assigns initial conditions to the RHS; uses node numbering \fBAssignInitialConditions()\fP writes the present value of the dependent \fBvariable(s)\fP of the computation into the righthand vector\&. It is applied only in transient calculations\&. \fBAssignInitialConditions()\fP makes use of the mesh and property storage managers\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
\fBAssignInitialConditions()\fP searches the dependent variable lists (basic operands) to retrieve those to accumulate them into the righthand vector\&. If there are multiple ones, the 'offset' variable is used to distinguish their placement\&. Dependent on the variable type (scalar, vector, tensor) the accumulation process differs since the degrees of freedom differ\&. Here the model dimension is used to determine the DOFs\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
\fBAssignInitialConditions()\fP is applied internally, when the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is passed to the \fBModel\fP object\&. Initial conditions are only assigned if the model is transient\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The following errors and warnings will be reported:

.PP
.PP
.nf
please call EstablishMatrixSetup() prior to this method\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP has not been executed so far\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
No basic operands have been specified\&.\&.\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
If there are no dependent variables defined !!!

.PP
.PP
.nf
Warning: So far no conditions are assigned to elements, faces, segments
.fi
.PP

.PP
The dependent variable must be placed on the nodes:

.PP
TODO: write alternative method that deals with the case when a boundary is only partially overlapping with the model domain\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::CoupleDomainsAcrossSplitBoundary (\fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & subdomain)\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
couples domains separated by SplitBoundaries using the information from NodeManifolds Loops over the nodes of the \fBRegion\fP \fBBoundary\fP\&. If these are manifolds, the coupling is applied\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
whether the test function variable should be coupled across the interface is determined from the value of the test-function operand at the SplitBoudary\&. If it is flagged ANY or PLAIN, lke at any no-flow boundary, no coupling is created, if it is ROBIN, the nodes in the manifold are coupled\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
