.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::EstablishMatrixSetup (const \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & gref)\fR [protected]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
resizes sparse solution matrix and establishes variable offsets if a system of equations will be solved \fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP involves the following steps:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
The basic sizes of the solution matrix, solution vector and righthand vector are determined\&.
.IP "2." 4
Each MathOperator has a basic, and a test variable operand\&. Basic operands are checked for their type and compared among operations to identify what size the final solution matrix G should have\&.
.IP "3." 4
Offset are assigned to individual MathOperators to indicate to what positions in the solution matrix they must accumulate to\&. This applies when multiple dependent variables are being solved for in the solution process\&.
.IP "4." 4
The solution matrix G, the solution vector x and the righthand vector rh are resized according to the operator specifications and the application target (\fBRegion\fP or \fBModel\fP)\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP takes information from the mesh and property managers and may query the property database for the type of variables\&. In the case of a group-restricted computation, the group interface is accessed via a pointer (gptr)\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
\fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP is applied internally when the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is passed to the \fBModel\fP\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP can terminate emitting messages when:

.PP
.PP
.nf
computational domain not specified"
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the target group could not be found\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator cannot add new variable to database
.fi
.PP

.PP
When you mispelled a variable name or if you tried to use a non-existant variable in your computation\&. \&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
righthand basic Operand's offset could not be resolved\&.\&.\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
Again, when a physical variable is unknown to the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP object such that it cannot resolve its \fBcsmp::Index\fP\&.

.PP
The following Error won't terminate \fBPDE_Integrator\fP application:

.PP
.PP
.nf
There is no test operand for basic operand: x csmp::Index: idx
.fi
.PP

.PP
Here, the finite-element interpolation functions do not model the dependent variable\&.

.PP
Warning messages that can arise during the execution of \fBEstablishMatrixSetup()\fP are the following:

.PP
.PP
.nf
PDE_Integrator was used before, using same specifications again\&.\&.\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
You forgot to call \fBReset()\fP before re-using an existing \fBPDE_Integrator\fP\&. You will now accumulate into the inverted solution matrix\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
lefthand basic operand not found
.fi
.PP

.PP
Each \fBPDE_Integrator\fP needs a lefthand basic operand in order to define a PDE problem\&. Define one before applying the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
There is no test operand for basic operand:
.fi
.PP

.PP
Your element interpolation functions do not model your dependent variable\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
Basic operand is an Element variable\&. Is this intended ?
.fi
.PP

.PP
Typically, the dependent variable in a finite-element computation, e\&.g\&., the basic operand is placed on the nodes\&. This lies in the very nature of the finite-element method\&. If you are doing a finite-volume or other computation, just ignore this warning\&.

.PP
TODO: consider case where one might want to retain the right-hand vector, but not the matrix TODO: rather than throwing the entire matrix away, one might just remove off-diagonal elements 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::FirstGuess (const std::vector< double > & guess)"

.PP
optional input of a solution vector with an initial guess of the result; for SAMG set ifirst=0 so that this vector is used Registers vector guess as first guess in the solution procedure for the algebraic multigrid solver\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use this method to supply a first guess solution different from the previous solution obtained with this algorithm\&.

.PP
NOTE: Make sure that you actually use an algebraic multigrid solver object and that the parameter ifirst in its \fBSAMG_Settings\fP object is set to 0\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> std::list< std::string > \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::IdentifySharedBoundaries (const \fBModel\fP< dim > & model, const \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & subdomain)\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
checks whether any \fBBoundary\fP object in the model is a surface of the computational domain Identifies the names of any model boundaries (standard and split ones), which are shared with the computational domain 'subdomain' by searching for the corresponding string in the boundaries\&. The identified boundaries are returned in the argument list\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
method assumes that the names of the boundaries are composed of the strings BOUNDARY, the name of the volumetric boundary that is adjacent to them and their name, for instance, BOUNDARY_MATRIX_LANDSURFACE\&. The only other names that are handled are those of the boundaries in a box-shaped model\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
method is costly so that it should not be repeatedly applied, but results should be stored\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
method also handles case, where the computational domain is the region '\fBModel\fP'\&.

.PP
internal model boundaries or edge boundaries are not handled yet\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
SKM 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::IntegrateOver (\fBModel\fP< dim > & model, \fBModelSubDomain\fP< dim, CELLTYPE > & domain, bool debug = \fRfalse\fP)"

.PP
also considers "dS" pde operators from \fBBoundary\fP or \fBSplitBoundary\fP objects if these share nodes with domain on which the solution is obtained Accumulates element integrals, but simultaneously considering potential \fBBoundary\fP objects associated with the simplicial complex\&. The domain is the computational domain to which the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is applied\&.

