.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::Reset (bool delete_math_operators = \fRtrue\fP)\fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
returns PDE integrator into the state created by default constructor ( 
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
is Reset is this needed?) 
.RE
.PP

.PP
After an \fBPDE_Integrator\fP has been applied, the solution matrix G and the righthand vector rh contain data which need to be removed before a new accumulation can take place\&. This is done automatically by the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP, unless it has been asked to retain the solution matrix\&.

.PP
However, you may want to use new PDE operators, a new computational domain and/or computational method\&. In this case, \fBReset()\fP allows to restore the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP's default state and zero's the elements of its matrix and vector storage\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Typically, you will reset an \fBPDE_Integrator\fP if you can just re-use it for another task\&. This is always recommended, since this permits to make use of the already initialized solution matrix, because the memory of it (owing to the Meschach implementation) is not de-allocated before the programme terminates\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::SetSolver (\fBSolver\fP & new_solver)"

.PP
switch to another solver deleting any dynamically allocated solver that was associated with integrator If a solver was allocated earlier it is deleted before the new_solver is connected to the \fBIntegrator\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::SolutionVector (std::vector< double > & sol) const"

.PP
outputs solution vector; 
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
this produces meaningful results only after application of the PDE integrator
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use this method to extract the solution vector from the algorithm, for instance to use it as initial guess in another time step\&. (Use method FirstGuess) 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::Solve ()\fR [protected]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
calls connected solver object to find x in G x = rh problem Calls the \fBSolver\fP interface SolveMatrixEquation() and hands G, rh, x to the \fBSolver\fP which returns the solution into the vector x\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The method \fBSolve()\fP is called internally, when the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP object is passed to the \fBModel\fP\&.

.PP
The solver reports a range of parameters in the solution process\&. These are described in detail in the CSMP User's guide\&. Important is that the final residue of the solution (which could be compared to a signal-to-noise ratio) is several orders of magnitude lower than the initial residue\&. Otherwise the obtained solution is useless\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> void \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::TimeIncrement (double dt)"

.PP
sets the time-increment for- and triggers a transient calculation (use 1/t if problem has been set up that way) Sets the time-icrement in a transient calculation\&.

.PP
If one forget to set the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP to transient, this is done as well\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Set the time-increment of an \fBPDE_Integrator\fP before you Apply() it to the \fBModel\fP or target \fBRegion\fP objects\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELLTYPE> bool \fBcsmp::PDE_Integrator_CRM\fP< dim, CELLTYPE >::Transient () const"

.PP
returns whether a finite-difference time increment has been set Transient \fBPDE_Integrator\fP

.PP
The accumulation process for a transient \fBPDE_Integrator\fP is different from a steady-state \fBPDE_Integrator\fP in that initial conditions must be assigned and matrices must be multiplied with the time_increment\&. This method sets the \fBPDE_Integrator\fP state to transient or retrieves its state\&.
